\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N$, $\mathbb Z$, $\mathbb Q$, $\mathbb R$, and $\mathbb C$ denote the natural numbers, integers, rationals, reals, and complex numbers, respectively. All additional parameters and hypotheses are specified in the question.


\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Establish the Fourier restriction conjecture and Kakeya dimension conjecture in their full dimensional ranges. For restriction, determine the expected bounds for restricting Fourier transforms to curved hypersurfaces. For Kakeya, must a set in $\mathbb R^n$ containing a unit line segment in each direction have dimension $n$? The source poses these as an incidence-theoretic program, including the quantitative incidence bounds that underpin the two conjectures.
\par\smallskip\textit{Formulation references:} \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]}, \href{https://doi.org/10.4171/owr/2023/25}{[S2]}.

\subsection{Short English statement}
Establish the Fourier restriction conjecture and Kakeya dimension conjecture in their full dimensional ranges. For restriction, determine the expected bounds for restricting Fourier transforms to curved hypersurfaces. For Kakeya, must a set in $\mathbb R^n$ containing a unit line segment in each direction have dimension $n$? The source poses these as an incidence-theoretic program, including the quantitative incidence bounds that underpin the two conjectures.

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{itemize}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems, record 30005541 (OWR-13750336-001). Catalogue attribution under CC BY 4.0.
\url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
\item[S2] Original problem formulation and mathematical context.
\url{https://doi.org/10.4171/owr/2023/25}.
\end{itemize}
\label{end:30005541}

